# Lesson 12 of the Plasm Koans. Each row is one koan: what the learner discovers,
# and the acceptance scenario of Plasm it teaches (feature: scenario).
Feature: About writing an aggregate
  An aggregate is written whole, as it is read: inserting an order inserts its lines, an update from an order read writes the difference by key (new lines inserted, changed ones updated, missing ones deleted), and deleting an order deletes its lines. A line's error is the order's.

  Prerequisites: lesson 10, lesson 09.

  Background:
    Given the koans database with its starting data

  Scenario Outline: <koan>
    Given the koan "<koan>" in this lesson
    Then it passes with its reference answer and fails until its blanks are filled
    And it teaches the acceptance scenario "<teaches>"

    Examples:
      | koan                                      | the learner discovers                                                                                       | teaches                                                                        | hint                                                                                                                                                                                                                  |
      | Inserting an order inserts its lines      | one Tx.insert of an Order writes the order and every line in its list                                       | owned-collections: Inserting an Invoice inserts its lines                      | Add the new order to the rows of the orders table under Given, and the lines cysOrder is built with to the rows of order_lines.                                                                                       |
      | The stored order holds its stored lines   | the returned Order holds its lines as stored, each with the new order's key                                 | owned-collections: Inserting an Invoice inserts its lines                      | Count the lines cysOrder is built with, under Given, and the comment says which order's key each stored line was given.                                                                                               |
      | Saved lines are read back                 | reading the order again reads the lines that were written                                                   | owned-collections: Inserting an Invoice inserts its lines                      | The line is read back as cysOrder built it: see the quantity and the price given to New.line under Given.                                                                                                             |
      | Adding a line to an order read            | a line without a key, added to the list, is inserted by the update                                          | owned-collections: Changing the lines of an Invoice read writes the difference | The comment says how many lines the order starts with, and the update adds the line appended to the list.                                                                                                             |
      | Lines that did not change are not written | an update that changes only the order's own fields sends nothing to order_lines but a read                  | typed-transactions: An update writes only the columns that changed             | The comment says what an update sends to order_lines when only the total changes, so the first order keeps the rows it has under Given.                                                                               |
      | Lines are matched by key                  | a line still in the list is updated in place and keeps its key                                              | owned-collections: Changing the lines of an Invoice read writes the difference | The first line keeps the key the comment gives it, wrapped as an OrderLineId, and has the price the with expression sets.                                                                                             |
      | A line left out of the list is deleted    | the list is the whole truth about the lines: a line removed from it is deleted, with no policy to configure | owned-collections: Changing the lines of an Invoice read writes the difference | Count the lines in the list the update writes: the comment says that list is the whole truth.                                                                                                                         |
      | Changing some lines is changing the list  | to remove some lines, filter the list in F#; the lines kept stay as they were                               | owned-collections: Changing the lines of an Invoice read writes the difference | Take the first order's rows in order_lines under Given, drop the Gadget's, and give the product_id of what is left as a ProductId.                                                                                    |
      | A line's error is the order's field error | a line's broken check comes back as the order's field error Lines[1].UnitPrice, and nothing is written      | constraints: A broken column check is the field's error                        | The error's Field is a path, made of the order's field, the failing line's place in the list in square brackets and counted from zero, a dot and the line's field, and the comment says what is written of the order. |
      | Deleting an order deletes its lines       | Tx.delete of an order read deletes the lines it owns                                                        | owned-collections: Deleting an Invoice deletes its lines                       | The comment says what Tx.delete does to the lines the order owns.                                                                                                                                                     |
